Full Kelly vs. Fractional Kelly Implementation
While Full Kelly ( $1.0 \cdot f^*$ ) yields optimal geometric growth under ideal theoretical conditions, it generates extreme portfolio volatility and severe equity drawdowns that are unmanageable in practice.
* Full Kelly (  $f^*$  ) : Maximizes terminal wealth but results in drawdowns often exceeding  50% to 60% . It assumes precise input accuracy and statistical trade independence—assumptions that rarely hold in volatile crypto markets.
* Half-Kelly (  $0.5 \cdot f^*$  ) : Captures approximately  75% of the maximum growth rate  while reducing portfolio volatility and drawdown depth by  50% . Half-Kelly is widely accepted as the standard risk benchmark for professional systematic traders.
* Quarter-Kelly (  $0.25 \cdot f^*$  ) : Allocates 25% of the full Kelly formula output. It produces smoother equity curves, accommodates estimation errors in win-rate metrics, and provides a conservative risk profile for live market operations.
