Converting Risk Fraction to Position Sizing
Once the optimal risk fraction ( $f$ ) is determined, convert it into standard order metrics:
$$\text{Dollar Risk Amount} = \text{Account Equity} \times f$$
$$\text{Position Size (Notional Units)} = \frac{\text{Dollar Risk Amount}}{\text{Entry Price} - \text{Stop-Loss Price}}$$
$$\text{Position Size (As % of Account)} = \frac{\text{Dollar Risk Amount}}{\text{Stop-Loss Distance as Decimal}}$$
Example : On a  $25,000 account using Half-Kelly (15% risk = ****$  3,750  dollar risk), planning a Bitcoin purchase with an entry at $50,000 and a hard stop at $49,000 (a  2%  stop distance):  $$\text{Notional Position Size} = \frac{\$3,750}{0.02} = \$187,500 \text{ worth of BTC}$$  On spot markets, this requires capital or leverage; on margin/futures exchanges, it dictates the required leverage ratio (7.5 $\times$  relative to account equity).
Portfolio Kelly Matrix for Correlated Assets
When deploying automated capital across multiple simultaneous crypto positions, trade independence ( $q = 1-p$ ) no longer holds due to market correlation. Calculating single-asset Kelly independently across correlated assets leads to overbetting. The multi-asset Kelly vector ( $\mathbf{f}^*$ ) must be solved using covariance matrices:
$$\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{C}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\mu}$$
* $\mathbf{C}^{-1}$ : Inverted covariance matrix of asset returns.
* $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ : Vector of expected excess strategy returns.
Limitations & Estimation Error Hazards
1. Input Sensitivity ("Garbage In, Garbage Out") : Kelly sizing depends directly on historical win-rate ( $p$ ) and payoff ( $b$ ) inputs. Evaluating inputs over small trade samples (<50 trades) yields unreliable statistics. Overestimating win rate by just 5% can push allocations past optimal levels and into high-risk drawdown territory.
2. Autocorrelation & Volatility Clustering : The Kelly formula assumes independent, identically distributed outcomes. Crypto returns frequently exhibit serial correlation, volatility clustering, and tail risk, violating standard Kelly assumptions and reinforcing the requirement for fractional Kelly sizing.
3. Negative Kelly Warning : If a Kelly calculation yields a  negative number , the strategy has a negative expected value ( $EV < 0$ ). This serves as an absolute systematic filter:  do not trade the strategy .
